<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis-Alexandre Berthier was a French marshal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s chief of staff from 1796 to 1814. He occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1798, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> was proclaimed, and organised the staff work behind Napoleon's campaigns. Napoleon made him Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> in 1806 and Prince of Wagram in 1809. He rallied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> in 1814 and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier.jpg" alt="Louis-Alexandre Berthier" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Augustin Catherine Pajou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis-Alexandre_Berthier.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Berthier was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 20 November 1753, the eldest of five surviving children of Lieutenant-Colonel Jean-Baptiste Berthier of the Corps of Topographical Engineers. Two of his brothers, César and Victor-Léopold, became generals in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. His father instructed him in the military art, and he entered the army as a lieutenant in 1772. From 1780 to 1783 he served as a staff officer under the Count of Rochambeau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. In July 1789 he became chief of staff of the Versailles National Guard and protected the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> from popular violence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1792 Berthier became a maréchal de camp and chief of staff to Marshal Nicolas Luckner. He met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> in March 1796 and was at once made chief of staff of the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His power of work, accuracy and mastery of detail made him the ideal chief of staff, and he remained Napoleon's most valued assistant for the rest of his career. In 1798 he entered Italy, organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> prisoner. Berthier supervised the pope's removal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a>, where he died after a hard journey.</p><p>Berthier joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, helped in the coup of 18 Brumaire in 1799 and became Minister of War for a time. In 1800 a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> brought the retrocession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>. Napoleon made him a Marshal of the Empire in 1804. In 1806 Napoleon deposed the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>, and Berthier became its prince, though he never visited it. He received the title Prince of Wagram in 1809. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 he advised against the advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s first abdication Berthier retired to his estate at Grosbois and made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>. When Napoleon returned in March 1815 he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and on 1 June 1815 he died in a fall from a window of the Neue Residenz. The window sill stood 1.2 metres from the floor, so an accident seems unlikely. According to some accounts he was assassinated by members of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a>, while others say he threw himself out, maddened at the sight of Russian troops marching to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It remains unclear whether it was suicide or murder.</p><p>Berthier was not a great field commander, and the French army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> suffered reverses when he held temporary command in 1809. He was nevertheless known for his organising skill and his ability to carry out the emperor's directions to the smallest detail. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> is quoted as saying that had Berthier been at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> he would not have met that misfortune.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis-Alexandre Berthier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Napoleon gave Neuchâtel to Marshal <b>Berthier</b> from 1806 to 1814.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-neuchatel">Principality of Neuchâtel</a></li><li><blockquote>After the French general Duphot was killed in a riot in Rome in December 1797, French troops under General <b>Berthier</b> occupied the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-republic-1798-1799">Roman Republic (1798–1799)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Versailles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.81,2.14&amp;year=1793" aria-label="Versailles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis-Alexandre Berthier"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5120 5949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5120 5949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.81,2.14&amp;year=1793">What was here in 1793? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
